Subject: [PATCH] tests/env: keep mscratch valid across RVTEST_GOTO_LOWER_MODE

RVTEST_GOTO_LOWER_MODE swaps the caller's T3 into mscratch to obtain the
M-mode save-area pointer, and only restores it four instructions later:

    csrrw  T3, CSR_MSCRATCH, T3    # T3 = save ptr, mscratch = orig T3
    SREG   T1, ...(T3)
    SREG   T2, ...(T3)
    SREG   T4, ...(T3)
    csrrw  T1, CSR_MSCRATCH, T3    # mscratch restored

Throughout that window mscratch holds an arbitrary caller value instead of
the save-area pointer. Any trap taken there runs the trap prologue's own
"csrrw sp, mscratch, sp", which faithfully adopts that value as its save
pointer.

Callers routinely reach this macro with an MMIO base in T3. InterruptsSSm-00
arms MTIMECMP two instructions earlier and still holds the CLINT/ACLINT base
(0x02004000 on our platform), so the handler writes its saved context into
device registers, loads a return address from one of them, and jumps to zero:
an unrecoverable instruction-access-fault loop (observed: >100k traps, test
times out).

This is not a race in practice. The interrupt that lands in the window is the
timer the test has just armed, so on an implementation whose store reaches the
device a cycle or two after the store instruction issues, it arrives at a fixed
offset and hits the window every run. Merely shrinking the window does not help;
we measured the interrupt landing precisely on the single remaining instruction.

Fix by never displacing mscratch. The swap existed only to free a register, so
stash T1 in mtval instead and read mscratch non-destructively. mtval is
architecturally dead at this point: it is only meaningful after a trap, and this
macro ends in an mret to a lower privilege mode.

Deliberately does NOT mask mstatus.MIE around the window. That also closes the
hole, but breaks tests that arm an interrupt immediately before entering the
lower mode and require it to be taken -- InterruptsSSm-00 sets MIE and arms the
timer for exactly that purpose, with a source comment saying so.

If reusing mtval is unacceptable, the alternative is to extend the save area with
one extra slot at a fixed offset; the requirement is only that mscratch remain
valid for the whole macro.

---
--- a/tests/env/rvtest_trap_handler.h
+++ b/tests/env/rvtest_trap_handler.h
@@ -726,12 +726,31 @@
         //     register (T3) to the M-mode save area so this macro clobbers
         //     nothing. T3 is swapped with mscratch so even T3's original value
         //     can be captured; it is kept as the save-area pointer across the mret and restored last.
-        csrrw  T3, CSR_MSCRATCH, T3               // T3 = M save area ptr, mscratch = orig T3
-        SREG   T1, goto_lower_sv_off+0*REGWIDTH(T3) // save T1
-        SREG   T2, goto_lower_sv_off+1*REGWIDTH(T3) // save T2
-        SREG   T4, goto_lower_sv_off+2*REGWIDTH(T3) // save T4
-        csrrw  T1, CSR_MSCRATCH, T3               // T1 = orig T3; mscratch = save area ptr (restored)
-        SREG   T1, goto_lower_sv_off+3*REGWIDTH(T3) // save orig T3
+        // ZERO-WINDOW variant: mscratch is never displaced.
+        //
+        // The original sequence swaps orig T3 into mscratch and only restores it
+        // four instructions later. A trap in that window makes the trap prologue's
+        // own "csrrw sp, mscratch, sp" adopt orig T3 as its save pointer; callers
+        // reach here with T3 holding an MMIO base (e.g. the ACLINT), so the
+        // handler writes its context into a device window and returns to a garbage
+        // address -- an unrecoverable trap loop. Shrinking the window is not
+        // enough: the interrupt that triggers it can land deterministically on the
+        // remaining instruction.
+        //
+        // mtval is used as the one-instruction stash so mscratch keeps the
+        // save-area pointer at all times. mtval is architecturally dead here (it
+        // is only meaningful after a trap, and this macro ends in an mret to a
+        // lower mode). Deliberately does NOT touch mstatus.MIE: masking would
+        // break tests that arm an interrupt immediately before this macro and
+        // require it to be taken (InterruptsSSm-00 does exactly that).
+        csrw   CSR_MTVAL, T1                      // stash T1; mscratch untouched
+        csrr   T1, CSR_MSCRATCH                   // T1 = M save area ptr
+        SREG   T2, goto_lower_sv_off+1*REGWIDTH(T1) // save T2
+        SREG   T4, goto_lower_sv_off+2*REGWIDTH(T1) // save T4
+        SREG   T3, goto_lower_sv_off+3*REGWIDTH(T1) // save orig T3
+        csrr   T2, CSR_MTVAL                      // recover orig T1
+        SREG   T2, goto_lower_sv_off+0*REGWIDTH(T1) // save orig T1
+        mv     T3, T1                             // T3 = save area ptr (macro contract)
 
         //---- Step 1: Set/clear mstatus.MPV (virtualization bit) ----
    .if     ((\LMODE\()==VUmode) || (\LMODE\()==VSmode))
